-- title:
--   Lemma EC4.1 — The complete performance-difference identity
-- statement:
--   Fix an optimal history-dependent multiplier, a dynamic fluid policy generated from an optimal fluid schedule, and any mixed comparison policy. Starting at the specified deterministic initial state, the Lagrangian-minus-policy gap equals
--   $$\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbb E\left[\lambda_t^*(h_t)(B_t(z_t)-C_t)+r_t(S_t,A_t)-r_t(S_t,a_t^\pi)+\mathbb E_t^{A_t}[V_{t+1}^\pi]-\mathbb E_t^{a_t^\pi}[V_{t+1}^\pi]\right].$$
--   Here the outer trajectory $(S_t,A_t)$ is generated by the dynamic fluid policy, $C_t$ is its actual total resource consumption, and the comparison action is drawn at that same state and history. Each inner expectation uses the corresponding actual transition law and retains the same signal-history memory for the continuation value. The comparison policy’s randomization is averaged explicitly. The resource penalty and both continuation expectations are all retained. The comparison policy need not satisfy the resource constraint.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: periods 1 through T and finite nonempty state and signal sets → periods 0 through T-1 with terminal T, and finite carriers numbered by Fin (size+1); reason: index renaming represents every finite nonempty carrier and changes no model
--   - encoding: finite-horizon expectations and maxima or minima → finite probability sums and real extrema of the operationally defined feasible value sets; reason: feasible action sets are finite and contain zero, and the finite-horizon LP and fluid feasible objectives have finite attained extrema on their stated domains
--   - encoding: current-signal notation abbreviating the fixed fluid or multiplier history → complete signal history is explicit, and fluid memory is computed from the fixed optimizer schedule; reason: the paper’s policies depend on this memory even when their displayed arguments suppress it
--   - encoding: normalized local action masses, undefined when the local mass is zero → arbitrary probability-distribution completions at zero local mass, with the printed ratio required at positive mass; reason: the source allows zero occupancies and repaired trajectories can reach them; no full-support or optimal-action-completion condition is added
--   - generalization: the comparison policy is written as a function of the current joint state and signal → it may additionally depend on the complete signal history; reason: ignoring the prefix recovers every source policy, and the same telescoping identity holds with the memory kept fixed
-- source:
--   Brown and Zhang (2021), Dynamic Programs with Shared Resources and Signals: Dynamic Fluid Policies and Asymptotic Optimality, DOI https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2021.2181, supplied July 28, 2021 artifact (published citation basis), Lemma EC4.1, printed EC-12 / PDF 50

import Definitions.Def_BrownSharedSignals_Fluid_GreedyPolicy

open scoped BigOperators
open BrownSharedSignals.Fluid

namespace BrownSharedSignals.Fluid

theorem lemma_ec4_1 {N T : ℕ} (M : Model N T)
    (lam : Multiplier M) (hlam : OptimalMultiplier M lam)
    (u : Schedule M) (p : LocalPolicy M) (hp : DynamicFluidPolicy M u p)
    (π : Policy M) (hπ : IsPolicy M π) :
    lagrangian M lam 0 (initialHistory M) M.initialState -
      policyValue M π 0 (initialHistory M) M.initialState =
    ∑ t : Fin T, stageExpectation M (independentPolicy M p) t.val
      (telescopingSummand M lam π t.val) := by sorry

end BrownSharedSignals.Fluid
